#[chapter/08/lore]{
  notes: [
    "The Watering Eye folds a face without stealing it — a want said into deep water still belongs to its wanter."
    "Ordinary desires resist wish fulfillment more than spectacular ones: thrones are cheap, supper agreements are advanced karmic problems."
    "The water names the debt in the egg's heat: an unspent wish standing warm above the fold, owing a coincidence to a stranger."
    "Remembrancers — chalk-pale fish no longer than a spoon — swim through the faces the Watering Eye has kept and fold them gently so they fit. One nosed the egg's reflection for an edge to f..."
    "On still nights the well-winch brings the bucket up holding a face instead of water. The potboy greets it and lets it back down, and the kitchen draws from the rain-butt; the soup tastes ..."
    "Every song in the Watering Eye is a winch song underneath — honey-slow, four beats a turn — so the bucket never knocks a face on the way up."
    "The Genie's resolution glass, which cannot see a want smaller than a loaf, found the potboy's want for the heel of the last loaf; of Boof it showed only wet stones and an egg. The torch-a..."
    "Every song the Watering Eye sings has the winch song underneath; the regulars say you can hear it if you stop clapping. When a too-quick reel set the bucket swinging, the regular laid a h..."
    "Finding his want-needle blank on Boof, the Genie had its case half open to mend it before he remembered the Rift clerks' rule — listen before you mend — and heard it ticking perfectly."
  ]
}

